								<div class="title" id="post-title-4777582">
																		“communards” is an inherently funny word, and should be normalized
															</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/gaffsey" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(6590029); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=6590029&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1607460417" alt="Gaffsey" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=6590029)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/gaffsey" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(6590029); return false;" >Gaffsey</a></span> <span class="odate time_1600455128 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">18 Sep 2020 18:52</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4777582">
									

<p>This is an article about the intersection of hippies and computers in the late 1960’s. Despite the popular image of hippies as a bunch of pot-smoking luddites, there was a strain of hippie thought, particularly among the “Back to the Earth” communards that emphasized the emancipatory potential of computers and cybernetics. It had a huge influence on the thinking of what eventually became the internet, and after that, Silicon Valley. Reality’s weird sometimes.</p>
<p>This entire article was basically an excuse to force myself to read a book that’s been on my shelf for a while (<em>From Counterculture to Cyberculture: Stewart Brand, the Whole Earth Network, and the Rise of Digital Utopianism</em> by Fred Turner). It may seem like I was laying it on a bit thick with Woody's letters, but if anything, I was downplaying the deep strangeness of letters published in <span style="text-decoration: line-through;">the Whole Earth Catalog</span> the Mother Earth Journal.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">to&nbsp;anyone&nbsp;upset&nbsp;or&nbsp;confused&nbsp;by&nbsp;the&nbsp;ending</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">you&nbsp;can’t&nbsp;get&nbsp;mad&nbsp;at&nbsp;me.&nbsp;as&nbsp;someone&nbsp;who&nbsp;likes&nbsp;getting&nbsp;mad&nbsp;on&nbsp;the&nbsp;internet,&nbsp;i’m&nbsp;disappointed&nbsp;too</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>The Only Game In Town canon was conceived of as an extension of the <a href="http://wanderers-library.wikidot.com/thearchivistslog">description of the Hanged King in the Wanderers’ Library</a>.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The being currently known as the Hanged King was at one time a resident of the Library. However, during its residence, the Library grew more and more insular, more and more depressive in its scope. These problems finally reached their apex toward the end of the 11th century, when a huge amount of knowledge was suddenly rent from the Library and lost. The fear of knowledge and personal growth in the outside world reached a height that took centuries to repair, and the remnants of these feelings continue even today.</p>
<p>The Hanged King itself is an ancient, unknowable thing with more names than the stars. The ancient Babylonian manuscripts from which it grew were destroyed, but there are more and more instances of its influence appearing. Some regard it as the original source of fear to appear in written words, while others claim it is far older.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This was a feature at conception, and was actually revealed to thunderous silence <a href="http://www.scpwiki.com/forum/t-625548/only-game-in-town-hub#post-3743565">a few years back</a></p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<br />
Thanks go to <span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/weizhong" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1777811); return false;"><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1777811&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1600455128" alt="weizhong" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1777811)" /></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/weizhong" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1777811); return false;">weizhong</a></span>, <span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/riemann" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1787775); return false;"><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1787775&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1600455128" alt="Riemann" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1787775)" /></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/riemann" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1787775); return false;">Riemann</a></span>, and <span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/rumetzen" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(209204); return false;"><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=209204&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1600455128" alt="rumetzen" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=209204)" /></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/rumetzen" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(209204); return false;">rumetzen</a></span> for their invaluable support as team members and critics. And many thanks to <span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/a-random-day" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1841781); return false;"><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1841781&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1600455128" alt="A Random Day" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1841781)" 