					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/croquembouche" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2893766); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=2893766&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1607460417" alt="Croquembouche" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=2893766)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/croquembouche" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2893766); return false;" >Croquembouche</a></span> <span class="odate time_1601595738 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">01 Oct 2020 23:42</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4786533">
									

<p><sup>Votes don't help the author, so I've vowed to always leave a comment with mine, at the very least whenever I rate an article negatively. If you feel the same, help out by leaving critique with more of your votes. Comment follows:</sup></p>
<p>I don't like seeing non-international specifics in articles - &quot;Pollensbee, CA&quot; without specifying (I assume) US rubs me the wrong way. It's also confusing because it's shortly followed by a British-style date.</p>
<p>I thought the letters were good. I expected the plot to be 'computer goes wrong' generally, but the cards don't necessarily prompt the community's actions - I like the thought that they're <em>just like that</em>, and the computer was either reflecting that or taking advantage of it.</p>
<p>The crosslink to 701 didn't mean anything to me, so it didn't work as a punchline.</p>
<p>I don't think the SCP itself was used as much as it could have been. It's a premise that works in any setting, not exclusively the setting that the story takes place in, and it might have been interesting to see what advice it had to give once it had been contained. I can appreciate that the story was about the setting and not the SCP, though, but if that's the case I wonder why the cards are included at all and not handwaved away as infohazardous. The story doesn't seem to require the SCP at all, beyond a couple of explicit mentions, and the two feel disconnected. I think I may have missed a point here, so it's a novote overall.</p>
<p>As a contest entry, I don't see how this relates to the previous entry, nor do I see how it sets up another - but I've no reason to assume that this team's entries form a series at all. Equally, this entry doesn't require any background knowledge, which is definitely in its favour.</p>

							</div>

								
